<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Le Quesnoy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Le Quesnoy is a small fortified town in the Nord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, formerly part of the County of Hainaut, where its counts had a castle. It passed to France by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> in 1659 and was refortified by Vauban. In November 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> troops captured it by scaling its ramparts with ladders, an action still commemorated in the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/le-quesnoy.jpg" alt="Le Quesnoy" width="320" height="536" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JackyM59 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%27H%C3%B4tel_de_ville_et_beffroi_du_Quesnoy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
